\begin{abstract}
\rev{Unsupervised analysis of the electrocardiogram (ECG) promises label-free discovery of cardiac structure, but which representation of the signal lets clustering recover clinical classes has not been measured under chance-corrected metrics. We benchmarked four representation families for unsupervised separation of sinus rhythm (SR) from atrial fibrillation (AF) on PTB-XL. The families were whole-record raw flattening, a convolutional autoencoder (conv-AE), compact hand-crafted rhythm and morphology features, and two frozen foundation-model embeddings (HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder). Evaluation was chance-corrected (adjusted Rand index, ARI) and patient-grouped, so that no patient appeared in more than one fold. It was repeated over ten seeds for the clusterers that depend on one. Each representation was bounded by a supervised ceiling, the accuracy a supervised classifier reaches on the same features. ECGFounder clustered best (ARI up to 0.885), and 16 interpretable hand-crafted features were competitive (0.711). The raw and conv-AE representations matched a majority-class assignment (ARI $\approx 0$), although the conv-AE reached a supervised ceiling of 0.678 against a majority baseline of 0.504. The raw ceiling was 0.519, barely above that baseline. The same ordering of the representations held on a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task. The silhouette score rewarded the degenerate one-cluster solution (silhouette 0.81 at ARI 0.000), so it cannot validate ECG clustering alone. Feature scaling also changed the outcome: the mean ARI of the hand-crafted features fell from 0.629 under standard scaling to 0.229 under quantile scaling. These chance-corrected controls, which locate where the raw and conv-AE representations fail, are the contribution, together with the measurement of each representation under deployment stress. Under heavy class imbalance the hand-crafted features overtook ECGFounder. Under federation across ten heterogeneous sites their ARI fell from 0.644 to 0.591 while that of ECGFounder fell from 0.885 to 0.612, and their extraction cost fifteen times less per record.}
\end{abstract}

\rev{We therefore benchmarked four representation families for unsupervised SR-versus-AF separation (sinus rhythm versus atrial fibrillation), with the controls the original project lacked. The ordering of the representations follows from the class definitions and is not the contribution. SR and AF differ by definition in the regularity of the rhythm, so it is expected that compact hand-crafted features and a large foundation model both recover the split. The benchmark adds what its controls reveal and how the representations behave under deployment stress. The contributions are empirical:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A clustering benchmark of raw, convolutional autoencoder (conv-AE), hand-crafted and two frozen foundation-model representations. The benchmark is chance-corrected, significance-tested and patient-grouped, so that no patient appears in more than one fold. Each representation is bounded by a supervised ceiling, the accuracy a supervised classifier reaches on the same features, and the ordering is shown stable across the assumed number of clusters.
\item Two reproducible methodological cautions that explain the original project's misleading numbers. Internal validity indices, which score a partition from the data alone without labels, reward the degenerate one-cluster solution. Feature scaling, the per-feature transformation applied before clustering, changes the result by a wide margin.
\item A deployment comparison under two independent stressors: heavy class imbalance, and federation across sites that differ sharply in arrhythmia prevalence. The 16 hand-crafted features overtake ECGFounder, the stronger foundation model on balanced data, under imbalance. Across the federated sites they keep a larger share of the adjusted Rand index (ARI) they reach without federation than either foundation model does.
\item A generalization check on a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task, the dataset the closest prior benchmark omits.
\end{enumerate}
\end{revblock}
\rev{We propose no new method, and we credit the separability-versus-clusterability phenomenon to prior work. Every statement about a foundation model is scoped to the two models tested, for the reasons given in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.}

\rev{We compared four representation families on PTB-XL (Table~\ref{tab:main}):}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Raw flattening} reproduced the original project's design on all twelve leads, since that design concatenated every lead. Each lead of the 1000-by-12 signal was reduced by a non-overlapping six-sample mean to 166 samples, and the leads were concatenated into one 1992-dimensional vector ($\sim$2000 dimensions). Its features are the sample amplitudes themselves, with no clinical model imposed on the signal. It carries amplitude and energy, and nothing aligns the samples of one record in time with those of another.
\item \textbf{Convolutional autoencoder} (conv-AE): a 1-D convolutional autoencoder with a 32-unit bottleneck, trained to convergence with a validation split, on all twelve leads resampled to 12 by 2500 and z-scored per lead. This is a properly specified reconstruction model, in contrast to the original collapsed dense autoencoder. Because its objective is reconstruction, the bottleneck keeps whatever carries signal energy, which need not be what separates the classes.
\item \textbf{Hand-crafted} (16 features): RR-interval statistics and irregularity, P-wave presence and amplitude, QRS width and amplitude, and beat-template consistency \cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}, extracted with NeuroKit2 \cite{neurokit2021}. The RR statistics were SDNN (the standard deviation of the RR intervals), RMSSD (the root mean square of successive RR differences), pNN50 (the proportion of successive RR differences above \mbox{50\,ms}), the coefficient of variation, RR entropy and mean heart rate. All 16 features were computed from the 1000 samples of lead II alone, on which R-peak detection and wave delineation were performed. Lead II is the standard rhythm lead, the one on which RR regularity and P-wave presence are read. Single-lead recordings are also the established setting for automated AF detection \cite{clifford2017af}. A single-lead definition further suited the two-lead MIT-BIH beats, where a twelve-lead definition would not apply. The representation measures directly the two quantities that make up the AF signature, RR irregularity and P-wave absence.
\item \textbf{Foundation embeddings} (frozen): mean-pooled hidden states of HuBERT-ECG (768-d) \cite{hubertecg2024} and the 1024-d pooled features of ECGFounder \cite{ecgfounder2024}, both used as fixed feature extractors with no fine-tuning. Both took all twelve leads, a lead set fixed by the published models. HuBERT-ECG received the first \mbox{5\,s} at \mbox{100\,Hz}, flattened lead-major into a 6000-sample vector. ECGFounder received the full strip resampled to 12 by 5000 for its \mbox{500\,Hz} input, z-scored once over the whole signal. This arm asks whether a feature space fixed by large-scale pretraining places the rhythm classes in separate regions.
\end{itemize}
\end{revblock}
\rev{On MIT-BIH the rhythm features do not transfer to single beats, so a raw beat window, a 21-dimensional beat-morphology and RR-context feature set, and the conv-AE were compared. The 21-dimensional set is a separate beat-level feature set of the same kind as the 16 PTB-XL features, not the same features. Neither foundation model was applied to the 2-lead beats, because both are tied to the 12-lead strip format. The two lead sets of the PTB-XL arms answer to different constraints. The hand-crafted arm was single-lead by clinical convention, and the raw arm was twelve-lead because that is the design it reproduces. The raw arm therefore received more input than the hand-crafted arm, so a lack of input is unlikely to explain its chance-level clustering. The twelve-lead input also made the raw vector 1992-dimensional, however, and that dimensionality may itself bear on Euclidean clustering.}

\rev{Each representation was clustered with five algorithms of scikit-learn \cite{sklearn2011}, with the number of clusters set to the number of classes:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{itemize}
\item $k$-means, with ten restarts per seed.
\item Ward agglomerative clustering, with Ward linkage on Euclidean distance.
\item Spectral clustering, with a nearest-neighbour affinity and $k$-means label assignment.
\item A Gaussian mixture model (GMM), with diagonal covariance and a covariance floor of \mbox{$10^{-4}$}, since a full covariance is singular for the high-dimensional representations.
\item Hierarchical density-based spatial clustering of applications with noise (HDBSCAN) \cite{hdbscan2013}, with a minimum cluster size of $n/50$ rounded down and floored at 10, which is 50 records on the PTB-XL cohort.
\end{itemize}
\end{revblock}
\rev{Tolerances and iteration caps were the library defaults, and no early stopping was imposed on the clusterers. Ward agglomerative clustering and HDBSCAN are deterministic given the input matrix, so each was fitted once rather than once per seed.}

\rev{The conv-AE was trained with Adam at a learning rate of \mbox{$10^{-3}$} and batch size 64, for at most 50 epochs on a 90/10 train/validation split. Training stopped when the validation reconstruction error had not improved for eight epochs, and the best-validation weights were kept. The two foundation models were used as frozen feature extractors with their published weights unchanged. HuBERT-ECG was mean-pooled over time from its last hidden layer at batch size 64, and the globally average-pooled 1024-dimensional feature layer of ECGFounder was taken at batch size 32.}

\rev{The headline metrics were the chance-corrected adjusted Rand index (ARI) \cite{hubertarabie1985} and adjusted mutual information (AMI) \cite{vinh2010}. Normalized mutual information (NMI), V-measure, Fowlkes-Mallows, purity, silhouette \cite{rousseeuw1987} and the largest-cluster fraction were reported alongside, and every metric was computed against a majority and a random baseline. Table~\ref{tab:metrics} gives each reported metric with its definition, its range, and how a value on it is read, in this notation:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{itemize}
\item $U$ is the partition by true class and $V$ the partition by cluster.
\item $n$ is the number of records, and $n_{ij}$ the number of records in class $i$ and cluster $j$.
\item RI is the fraction of record pairs on which $U$ and $V$ agree.
\item $I$ and $H$ are mutual information and entropy, with $\bar{H}$ the arithmetic mean of $H(U)$ and $H(V)$.
\item $E[\cdot]$ is the expectation under the permutation model that holds both sets of group sizes fixed.
\item $a_k$ and $b_k$ are the mean distance from record $k$ to the rest of its own cluster and to the nearest other cluster.
\end{itemize}
\end{revblock}
\rev{None of the eight metrics carries a physical unit. The first five are dimensionless indices of agreement between the true partition and the recovered one, purity is the majority-class share inside the clusters, the silhouette is a dimensionless index of cluster compactness computed without labels, and the largest-cluster fraction is a proportion of the cohort.}

\rev{A supervised ceiling bounded how much of the label a probe recovers from the same features under the same patient-grouped five-fold protocol. We reported both a linear probe (balanced logistic regression) and a non-linear probe (random forest), so that a high ceiling with a low ARI identifies a geometric rather than an informational failure. Each configuration was repeated over ten seeds, and the headline contrasts were tested with a paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test across seeds.}

\rev{Because heavy-tailed features can dominate Euclidean distance, we swept four feature-scaling methods (Yeo-Johnson power, standard, quantile, robust) and report power scaling as the default unless stated otherwise. Feature scaling was fitted on the full representation matrix, and any hand-crafted feature that a record left undefined was filled with that feature's cohort median before scaling. To check that fixing the cluster count to the number of classes does not flatter the comparison, we also report adjusted mutual information, which is comparable across cluster counts, for $K$ from 2 to 8. Because the natural SR-to-AF prevalence is imbalanced, we additionally evaluated on a 9:1 SR:AF subsample.}

