\rev{The practical reading is a decision rule for the two tasks evaluated here: SR-versus-AF rhythm separation on PTB-XL and N-vs-V beat morphology on MIT-BIH. If a strong public foundation model can be applied to balanced data in the target 12-lead format, ECGFounder gives the best unsupervised separation here. If the data are imbalanced or siloed across sites, if interpretability or low dimensionality matters, or if the leads on hand are not the standard twelve, the 16 hand-crafted features are the better choice. On the PTB-XL cohort they come within reach of ECGFounder on balanced data, overtake it under the 9:1 imbalance (ARI 0.265 against 0.063), and vary far less over the site draws of non-IID federation.}

\rev{The cost comparison of Table~\ref{tab:cost} supports the same rule. The representation that retains the most ARI under both stressors is also the less costly of the two to compute, and its 16 coordinates are clinical quantities that a reader can inspect for each record. Choosing the hand-crafted features for their behaviour under imbalance or federation therefore costs neither compute nor interpretability.}

\rev{The lead constraint is concrete. Neither foundation model tested could embed the 2-lead MIT-BIH beats, whereas the single-lead hand-crafted design carried over to them through a separate beat-level feature set of the same kind. The raw and conv-AE representations, including the original project's design, are not viable for label-free arrhythmia structure on these tasks. On PTB-XL they were indistinguishable from predicting the majority class under all five clusterers. On MIT-BIH their best ARIs were 0.127 for the conv-AE and 0.049 for the raw beat window, against 0.783 for the beat-morphology features.}

\rev{Six things separate this study from the closest ECG foundation-model clustering benchmark \cite{lookingbeyond2026}:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A hand-crafted axis turns a comparison between foundation models into a comparison between representation families.
\item A supervised ceiling on every representation, linear and non-linear, tells a space that does not hold the label apart from one that holds it in the wrong geometry.
\item An out-of-fold test of that distinction, in which a discriminant fitted on other patients raises HuBERT-ECG from 0.160 to 0.549 in held-out ARI, shows that the second case occurs.
\item A second dataset and a second task type, the MIT-BIH beat morphology that benchmark omits, test whether the ordering of the representations depends on the task.
\item Two cautions that a practitioner can act on concern the degenerate silhouette and the dependence of the result on feature scaling.
\item Two deployment stressors, with the cost of each representation beside them, show where the ordering on balanced data stops being the useful answer.
\end{enumerate}
\end{revblock}
\rev{The components themselves are existing tools: the separability-versus-clusterability phenomenon is known \cite{sslclustering2025}, and the federated procedure is standard \cite{fedavg2017}.}

\rev{ECGFounder expects \mbox{500\,Hz} input, and because bulk access to the \mbox{500\,Hz} records was limited, the main benchmark embedded the \mbox{100\,Hz} cohort upsampled to the expected length. A check at \mbox{500\,Hz} used a 300-record subset in place of the full cohort. On that subset ECGFounder reached an ARI of 0.896 at native \mbox{500\,Hz} and 0.909 on upsampled \mbox{100\,Hz} input, a difference of 0.013 in favour of the upsampled input. The main benchmark may therefore overstate the ARI of ECGFounder by about that amount, which is small against its lead of 0.242 over the hand-crafted features.}

\rev{We evaluated two foundation models, and other foundation models, including some reported to cluster well \cite{lookingbeyond2026}, were not included. The input of one of the two was also shorter than that of every other arm. HuBERT-ECG received the first \mbox{5\,s} of each \mbox{10\,s} strip and so saw fewer RR intervals, while every other arm saw the full strip. The shorter window is therefore an alternative explanation for its weak clustering, beside the off-axis geometry measured in Section~\ref{sec:results} and the organisation by dataset that \cite{lookingbeyond2026} reported for some foundation models.}

\rev{Both evaluated tasks are binary by design, one rhythm contrast and one morphology contrast, each chosen so that the class definition is unambiguous and a clustering failure can be charged to the representation rather than to label noise. No multi-class arrhythmia cohort was evaluated, and no result here is a statement about multi-class arrhythmia structure, where several rhythms share an irregularity signature and the largest class outnumbers every minority. A multi-class PTB-XL rhythm cohort over the same embeddings is the least costly way to test whether the ordering survives that setting. Within the binary rhythm task the AF class also counted atrial flutter records as positives. Flutter need not show the irregular rhythm on which the reading of Section~\ref{sec:intro} rests, so those records may sit closer to the SR class than the AF definition suggests. No foundation model was evaluated on MIT-BIH, so the MIT-BIH results compare the hand-crafted, conv-AE and raw representations only.}

\rev{The federated study is a simulation on a single physical dataset, and no multi-institution deployment was evaluated. Its only control on heterogeneity is the Dirichlet concentration, which moves class prevalence across sites and nothing else, and the spread that alone produces is already wide: over the ten redrawn non-IID partitions ECGFounder ranged from chance to 0.881, while the hand-crafted features stayed between 0.455 and 0.643. Feature scaling in the simulation also used statistics pooled over all sites, which separate institutions would not share, and the choice of scaling changed the centralized results by a wide margin (Section~\ref{sec:results}). Device vendor, lead placement, population case mix and local labelling practice also differ between hospitals and are absent from the simulation. Settling their effect needs recordings held at several institutions, clustered under a federated protocol with the site of origin retained as a variable, which was not available for this study.}
